Remember the difference between cloud-enabled and cloud-native? One group lifted their old apps onto AWS and called it a day. The other rebuilt from scratch. Looked the same for a year. Five years later you couldn't compare them.

We're doing it again. With AI.

The companies actually getting somewhere aren't the ones with the boldest memo or the biggest tool budget. They're the ones treating this as intentional and patient work. Not a project. Not an initiative with a finish line. Not something you announce, deploy, and tick off.

Because AI isn't a tool you adopt. It's a way of working you grow into. It has to end up in how decisions get made, how teams are shaped, how problems get framed before anyone starts on them.

The work itself is unglamorous, but it usually comes with a set of questions to ask yourself (in order):

1. Are people actually using or trying to use the tools?
2. Are they actually getting more done?
3. Can the you rest of the company handle the new speed? What tools can we use to mitigate this?
4. Repeat

<br />

1. Can the rest of the company decide what to work on fast enough to keep up?
2. Are you sure that you have the capacity to build your
3. Are you building your own internal tools for all this — and replacing them when they get stale?
4. And honestly: how should teams even be organized now? Nobody has figured this out yet.

<br />

If you are not getting past #1 or #2, you should really question your or your companies efforts. Because things starts to get real interesting in #3.

Most companies get stuck at #3. The team moves fast, but the company keeps pointing it at the wrong things.

One detail worth stealing — it's from an engineering team, but the idea generalizes: at Chainguard, managers are expected to use AI about as much as the people they manage do on average. Use it less and you can't coach the people who use it more. Use it way more and you should be teaching, not keeping the trick to yourself. Small policy. Says a lot about how seriously they're taking this.

None of this makes for good headlines. The bold memo gets the press. The walkback gets the press. The slow, deliberate, never-quite-done part in the middle doesn't.

That's probably how you know it's the part that matters.
